1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
How can your high beams stay on
without blinding the driver ahead?

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,100
With ordinary high beams,
lighting up the road

3
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,300
can also mean shining straight
into another driver's mirrors.

4
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:13,100
Some adaptive headlights solve this
by splitting the beam into sections.

5
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:15,300
A camera spots the car ahead.

6
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:19,700
Then, a controller dims the sections
aimed toward it, while the rest stay bright.

7
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,500
As the car moves,
that gap moves with it,

8
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:26,300
so the road around it stays lit,
with less glare aimed at the other driver.

9
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:28,100
It looks like a moving shadow.

10
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:31,500
But the trick is choosing
where the light doesn't go.

11
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:35,500
Like and subscribe to discover
how everyday things really work.
